<title>UKRAINE: SOCCER-UKRAINIAN LEAGUE CHAMPIONS SINCE 1992.</title>
<headline>SOCCER-UKRAINIAN LEAGUE CHAMPIONS SINCE 1992.</headline>
<dateline>KIEV 1997-05-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>Dynamo Kiev, the most successful</p>
<p>club in Soviet soccer history, secured their fifth successive</p>
<p>Ukrainian league title on Friday.</p>
<p>Title winners and runners-up since the Ukrainian</p>
<p>championship was formed in 1992 after the collapse of the Soviet</p>
<p>Union (1992 spring only, 1992-93 onward autumn-spring):</p>
<p>		Champions			 Runners-up</p>
<p> 1992	 Tavria Simferopol	     Dynamo Kiev</p>
<p> 1992-93    Dynamo Kiev		     Dnipro Dnipropetrovsk</p>
<p> 1993-94    Dynamo Kiev		     Shakhtar Donetsk</p>
<p> 1994-95    Dynamo Kiev		     Chornomorets Odessa</p>
<p> 1995-96    Dynamo Kiev		     Chornomorets Odessa</p>
<p> 1996-97    Dynamo Kiev		     To be decided</p>
<p>Three Ukrainian clubs won the old Soviet championship.</p>
<p>Dynamo Kiev, who in 1961 became the first side from outside</p>
<p>Moscow to win it, went on to claim a record 13 titles, in 1961,</p>
<p>1966, 1967, 1968, 1971, 1974, 1975, 1977, 1980, 1981, 1985, 1986</p>
<p>and 1990.</p>
<p>Dnipro Dnipropetrovsk won two Soviet titles, in 1983 and</p>
<p>1988. Zarya Voroshilovgrad (now Lugansk) were champions in 1972.</p>
<p>The following clubs were Soviet champions from the founding</p>
<p>of the league in 1936 to the collapse of the Union in 1991:</p>
<p> Dynamo Kiev  (Ukraine)			 13 times</p>
<p> Spartak Moscow  (Russia)		     12</p>
<p> Dynamo Moscow  (Russia)			11</p>
<p> CSKA Moscow  (Russia)			   7</p>
<p> Torpedo Moscow  (Russia)			3</p>
<p> Dnipro Dnipropetrovsk  (Ukraine)	    2</p>
<p> Dynamo Tbilisi  (Georgia)		     2</p>
<p> Ararat Yerevan  (Armenia)		     1</p>
<p> Dynamo Minsk  (Belarus)			 1</p>
<p>Zarya Voroshilovgrad (now Lugansk) (Ukraine)  1</p>
<p>Zenit Leningrad (now St Petersburg) (Russia)  1</p>
